2.

The coordinates of a star are $\alpha_0 = 6^\mathrm{h}40^\mathrm{m}$, $\delta_0 = 23.5^\circ$. The distance to the star is $r_0 = 20$ pc. The star has a proper motion $\mu_\alpha = 0''$/yr and $\mu_\delta = 0.74''$/yr. The radial velocity of the star is $v_r = -39$ km/s. Determine:

– the coordinates of the star when it has receded from us to infinity;

– the time after which the star will be at the present celestial pole;

– the coordinates of the star at the moment of minimum approach.

Do not take into account the precession of the Earth's axis or the motion of stars within the Galaxy. (V. B. Ignatyev)

5.

On 26 September 2022 Jupiter was at a "great" opposition (the closest to the Sun and the Earth) and approached the Earth to a record small distance for the last 59 years, 591.3 million km. This is a unique chance to obtain the most detailed photographs of the planet's atmosphere. An amateur astronomer decided to shoot the planet with a telescope with an objective diameter $D_T = 150$ mm and a focal ratio of 1:5, using a three-times Barlow lens (BL, which increases the effective focal length 3 times) and an astrocamera with a sensor pixel size of $2.4\times2.4$ μm. Assume that Jupiter revolves around the Sun in the plane of the ecliptic. Determine:

a) the scale (in arcseconds per pixel) and the limit of the resolving power of the optical system "telescope–camera" (for the wavelength $\lambda = 555$ nm). Assume that the atmospheric distortions during the observation are not less than $1.5''$;

b) the maximum permissible exposure for one frame of the video at which there will still be no blurring of the planet's image as a result of the diurnal motion of the Earth (with the telescope tube fixed);

c) the optimal dimensions of one frame for shooting Jupiter using the BL, out of the possible formats ($1936\times1096$, $1280\times960$, $640\times480$, $968\times548$, $320\times240$ pixels), with the aim of minimising the file size;

d) the maximum duration of the video from whose frames an image of the planet can still be assembled without blurring of details caused by the axial rotation of Jupiter. Assume that the video is shot in a mode of accurate tracking of Jupiter by the telescope tube on a robotic mount, compensating the diurnal rotation of the Earth. (Yu. P. Filippov)

2. Explosion of a non-black body

Nova outbursts are explained by explosive synthesis of helium from hydrogen taking place on the surface of a white dwarf that is one of the components of a binary star. Suppose that an outburst occurred on a white dwarf with a mass equal to the mass of the Sun and a radius equal to the radius of the Earth, as a result of which the apparent magnitude of the star changed by $10^\mathrm{m}$ and at maximum brightness was $+10^\mathrm{m}$. It is known that as a result of the outburst 1% of the mass of the hydrogen envelope was converted into helium, the total energy radiated in the outburst was $10^{38}$ J, and the outburst lasted 11 days, during which the brightness of the nova practically did not change. Estimate:

A. the gravitational acceleration at the surface of the white dwarf;

B. the mass of the exploded hydrogen envelope of the white dwarf (in solar masses);

C. the distance to the nova;

D. the characteristic temperature of the hydrogen envelope immediately before the outburst;

E. the characteristic thickness of the hydrogen envelope immediately before the outburst.

In the reactions of synthesis of helium from hydrogen, 0.7% of the mass of the reacted matter is converted into energy. Take all bolometric corrections to be zero; neglect interstellar absorption.

4. Intergalactic surveillance

From the surface of the Earth at latitude $60^\circ$ N an elliptical galaxy consisting of 50 billion Sun-like stars is observed. The declination of the galaxy is $60^\circ$, and its redshift is 0.01. The galaxy is observed in a "window of transparency". Estimate

A. the distance to the galaxy;

B. the absolute magnitude of the galaxy;

C. the minimum and maximum altitudes above the horizon at which it is observed;

D. the minimum and maximum apparent magnitude of the galaxy during the year.

The atmospheric absorption at the zenith is $0^\mathrm{m}.2$.
